## Manual verification against live Bedrock

Ran an end-to-end session with this branch against real Bedrock in `ap-southeast-2` (SSO credentials) and confirmed a successful completion.

Config used:

```json
{
  "providers": {
    "bedrock": {
      "aws_region": "ap-southeast-2",
      "models": [
        { "id": "au.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0", "name": "Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Bedrock AU)", "context_window": 200000, "default_max_tokens": 8192, "can_reason": true, "supports_attachments": true },
        { "id": "au.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0", "name": "Claude Haiku 4.5 (Bedrock AU)", "context_window": 200000, "default_max_tokens": 8192, "can_reason": true, "supports_attachments": true }
      ]
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "large": { "provider": "bedrock", "model": "au.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0" },
    "small": { "provider": "bedrock", "model": "au.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0" }
  }
}
```

`crush run -q "..."` returned the expected output. Requests hit `bedrock-runtime.ap-southeast-2.amazonaws.com`, confirming `aws_region` routes correctly — on `main` the same config still goes to `us-east-1`.

A few field notes that may help other users (and argue for keeping this opt-in + explicit model IDs rather than auto-deriving them):

- **Inference-profile prefix matters and is region/policy sensitive.** A `apac.` profile can fan the actual invocation out to any APAC region (an early test resolved the model to `ap-northeast-1`), which an org Service Control Policy may deny. `au.`-prefixed profiles keep inference within Australia. Users pick the prefix that matches their region and policy.
- **Model availability differs by prefix.** In `ap-southeast-2`, Claude Haiku 4.5 is published under `au.`/`global.` but not `apac.`. So the region setting and the model IDs need to be chosen together.

Net: the region plumbing works; remaining constraints are AWS-side (profile availability + account policy), not Crush.

